                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#4SphereAsFlatOrbifold"} 4-Sphere
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#flat_compact_6dimensional_orbifolds"} Flat compact 6-dimensional orbifolds
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#7dimensional_orbifolds"}
                    7-Dimensional
                    = succeed "-orbifolds" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi G
                              %mn 2
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_2
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#general"} General
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#flat_orbifolds"} Flat orbifolds
                %ul
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#of_dimension_2"} Of dimension 2
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#of_dimension_3"} Of dimension 3
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#of_dimension_4"} Of dimension 4
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#of_dimension_6"} Of dimension 6
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The concept of
            %em Riemannian orbifolds
            is the joint generalization of the concepts of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifolds"} Riemannian manifolds
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifolds"} orbifolds
          %p
            A Riemannian orbifold is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold"} orbifold
            equipped with an orbifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atlas"} atlas
            where each
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chart"} chart
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi U
                      %mo ^
                    %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\widehat{U}_i, G)
            is equipped with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metric"} Riemannian metric
            such that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isometries"} isometries
          %p
            A key aspect is that the orbifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singularities"} singularities
            behave like carrying singular
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+orbifolds"} flat orbifolds
            (also “Euclidean orbifolds”, i.e. Riemannian orbifolds with vanishing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+curvature"} Riemann curvature
            away from the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singularities"} singularities
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-spheres"} n-spheres
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#FlatCompact2DimensionalOrbifolds"} below
          %p
            Key examples of flat orbifolds are global
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+quotients"} homotopy quotients
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝕋
                    %mi n
                  %mo ⫽
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}^n \sslash G
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-torus"} n-torus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝕋
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}^n
            equipped with its canonical flat
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metric"} Riemannian metric
            = succeed "." do
              %em toroidal orbifolds
          %p#ArtisticIllustrations
            Artistic illustrations of 2-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+orbifolds"} flat orbifolds
            (arising as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+orbifold"} quotients
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plane"} plane
            by
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wallpaper+groups"} wallpaper groups
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#CBG08"} Conway, Burgiel &amp; Goodman-Strauss 2008
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 10px 20px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/CBG-3star3-orbifold.png", :width => "570px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                (from
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#CBG08"} CBG 2008 p. 114
          %p
            %br/
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 10px 20px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/CBG-2222-orbifold.png", :width => "670px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                (from
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#CBG08"} CBG 2008 p. 116
          %h2#properties Properties
          %blockquote
            %p under construction
          %p
            Every flat orbifold whose underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+topological+space"} connected
            and
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+metric+space"} complete
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Ratcliffe06"}> Ratcliffe 06, 13.3.10
            )
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#noncompact_orbifolds Non-compact orbifolds
          %p
            Basic examples of non-compact Riemannian orbifolds are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conical+singularities"} conical singularities
          %p
            In the flat case these are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+quotients"} homotopy quotients
            of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⫽
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V\sslash G
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+group"} finite group
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi RO
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \in RO(G)
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+space"} finite-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+group"} orthogonal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+representation"} linear representation
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %div{:style => "text-align: center"}
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/Z2OrbifoldSingularity.jpg", :width => "600"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics grabbed from
              %a{:href => "#BlumenhagenLustTheisen13"} Blumenhagen-Lüst-Theisen 13
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℍ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V = \mathbb{H}
            equipped with the canonical action of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+subgroups+of+SU%282%29"} finite subgroups of SU(2)
            these are the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ADE-singularities"} ADE-singularities
          %h3#CompactFlatOrbifoldsFromCrystallographicGroups Compact flat orbifolds from crystallographic groups
          #CompactFlatOrbifoldFromCrystallographicGroup.num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+orbifolds"} flat orbifolds
                from
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystallographic+groups"} crystallographic groups
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean space
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi Iso
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \subset Iso(E)
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystallographic+group"} crystallographic group
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} acting
              on it, with translational
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+subgroup"} normal subgroup
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+%28discrete+subgroup%29"} lattice
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \subset S
              and corresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point+group"} point group
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                      %mo =
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G = S/N
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mn 1
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mn 1
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mtext normal subgroup
                              %mrow
                                %mtext lattice of translations
                          %mtd
                            %mi N
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⊂
                          %mtd
                            %mi E
                          %mtd
                            %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mtext translation
                              %mrow
                                %mtext group
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mtext crystallographic
                              %mrow
                                %mtext group
                          %mtd
                            %mi S
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⊂
                          %mtd
                            %mi Iso
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi E
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mtext Euclidean
                              %mrow
                                %mtext isometry group
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mtext point
                              %mrow
                                %mtext group
